From 97c177a33cf370781d40a748318a38c99a70dd02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mjb-is <mjb-is@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 01:27:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 70/76] machinebackup: skip hashing blank (all-zero) chunks

Every 4 MiB chunk of a machine image is SHA-256 hashed to get its digest,
even when it is all zeros. Blank space is a large share of most disks
(unpartitioned space, never-written or trimmed areas, the tail of a big
disk), and on a CPU without SHA instructions hashing a chunk takes 10 to
20 ms, so a mostly empty disk spent most of its run hashing zeros whose
digest is always the same.

uploadWorker now works out the digest of a zero chunk once, with the
client's own ChunkDigest (so an encrypted backup, which keys its digests
with the id_key, gets its own correct value), and any full-size chunk
that is all zeros takes that digest without being hashed. bytes.Equal
against a zero buffer stops at the first non-zero byte, so a chunk with
data costs almost nothing extra. Uploads, indexes and images are
unchanged: the digest is the same value hashing would give.

zero_chunk_test.go checks the short cut gives the hashed digest for plain
and encrypted clients, and that a chunk with one non-zero byte (first,
middle, last) or a short final chunk is still hashed. Benchmark on an
i5-7200U: 0.88 ms for a blank chunk against 18.1 ms to hash it.

